","difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"The increasing order of the first ionization enthalpies of the elements B, P, S and F (lowest first) is :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"F &lt; S &lt; P &lt; B "},{"identifier":"B","content":"P &lt; S &lt; B &lt; F"},{"identifier":"C","content":"B &lt; P &lt; S &lt; F "},{"identifier":"D","content":"B &lt; S &lt; P &lt; F "}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"The correct order of ionisation enthalpies is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.09ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.621ex\" height=\"1.686ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 6904.7 745\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D439\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D439\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1026.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(2082.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3111.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4167.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5089.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"3E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(6145.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>\n<br><br><b>NOTE :</b> On moving along a period ionization enthalpy increases from left to right and decreases from top to bottom in a group. But this trend breaks up in case of atom having fully or half filled stable orbitals. \n<br><br>In this case <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.699ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 751 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D443\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D443\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> has a stable half filled electronic configuration hence its ionisation enthalpy is greater in comparison to <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.088ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 923 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(645,0)\"><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> For B(5) electronic configuration is = 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sup>1</sup> and P(15) electronic configuration is = 1s<sup>2</sup>2s<sup>2</sup>2p<sup>6</sup>3s<sup>2</sup>3p<sup>3</sup>. As P has half filled valence shell so removing electron will be harder compare to B.  Hence by checking all options the correct possible order is\n<br><br>B &lt; S &lt; P &lt; F"}},"permalink":"the-increasing-order-of-the-first-ionization-enthalpies-of-t-2006-marks-4-fivb9zmmm2eisnsx.htm","timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"aieee-2006","topic":"ionization-energy","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":["sequence_ordering"],"yearKey":"aieee-2006","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"xHdlQU9vRCF2N2Dt","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"d-and-f-block-elements","year":2006,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2006","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Lanthanoid contraction is caused due to :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"the appreciable shielding on outer electrons by 4f electrons from the nuclear charge "},{"identifier":"B","content":"the appreciable shielding on outer electrons by 5d electrons from the nuclear charge "},{"identifier":"C","content":"the same effective nuclear charge from Ce to Lu "},{"identifier":"D","content":"the imperfect shielding on outer electrons by 4f electrons from the nuclear charge "}],"correct_options":["D"],"answer":null,"explanation":"The main reason of lanthanide contraction is, poor shielding of 4f electrons on the outer most shell electrons. As f orbital is defused nature so shielding of 4f electron is imperfect that is why nucleus can attract outer most shell electron easily and the size of atom decreases.\n<br><br>In lanthanides, there is poorer shielding of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.314ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1023 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> electrons by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.376ex\" height=\"2.059ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 1050 910\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> electrons resulting in greater attraction of the nucleus over <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.314ex\" height=\"1.62ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1023 716\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D451\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D451\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> electrons and reduction of the atomic radii."}},"permalink":"lanthanoid-contraction-is-caused-due-to-2006-marks-4-xhdlqu9vrcf2n2dt.htm","timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"aieee-2006","topic":"inner-transition-elements-lanthanoids-and-actinoids","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":[],"yearKey":"aieee-2006","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"MqWmPgL8oGjxUdMh","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"p-block-elements","year":2006,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2006","difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Which of the following statements is true? ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"H<sub>3</sub>PO<su